Captain hold: review round 1 - decision required

Purpose
Keep the context-size reminder visible by stopping automatic Claude turns until the captain explicitly dismisses the hold or starts a new session.
Ordinary replies must keep the hold active and ask for /stow then /clear, or dismissal.
Queued events must remain available after release.

Work preserved
The implementation is committed as 4adbf41 on fm/fm-captain-hold-marker.
It adds the hold command, checks in both Claude Stop paths, a captain-message reminder hook, new-session clearing after verified ownership, documentation, and deterministic regression cases.
No remote push, PR, merge, delivery pipeline, or change under ~/.claude was performed.
The branch is not ready to land because review found two problems.

Round history
Round 1 independently ran the three affected suites, examined four review lenses, and confirmed that all eight deliberately broken production-copy variants were caught by the tests.
It reproduced the two problems below and made no fixes.
The worker's combined run of five scripts also passed, covering auto-arm, turn-end guard, session start, startup nudges, and durable wake queues.
Targeted pinned ShellCheck, Bash syntax, whitespace, and documentation-audience checks passed.
The full suite and live Claude integration were deliberately not run under this task's targeted, local-only scope.
All reviewer experiments used isolated copies, and the reviewed production files were restored unchanged.

Finding 1 - an automated routed message gets treated as a captain reply
Example input: firstmate routes "Handle routed work" to a second mate while that home's hold is active.
Expected: the captain-only reminder hook says nothing for that automated input.
Actual: it adds instructions to ask the captain for /stow then /clear or dismissal.
The hook excludes the two required invisible prefixes, but misses the repository's additional routed-message prefix.
Likelihood: certain when a held second mate receives a routed message; irrelevant when no such message is delivered.
Who it hurts: the second mate and the captain, through an unnecessary decision request that can distract from or delay the routed work.
Severity: moderate, not data loss.
Fix cost: likely a small exclusion using the existing canonical message classifier, with an isolated regression case; estimate 5-10 minutes before review.
Reviewer identifier: R1-1, P2; bin/fm-captain-hold-prompt.sh:18.

Finding 2 - a late hold can still allow the very automatic turn it should stop
Example sequence: the hook checks "no hold"; the context reminder then sets the hold while the hook prepares its final response; the hook still requests another automatic turn.
Expected result: silent success, leaving the reminder visible.
Actual reproduced result: both the auto-arm and turn-end guard return exit 2 with a continuation banner even though the hold already exists.
A queued event survived the auto-arm reproduction, so the problem is unwanted continuation rather than lost events.
Likelihood: a timing-dependent overlap in normal use, not every Stop; the reviewer reproduced both paths deterministically by setting the hold at a command boundary without waiting.
Who it hurts: the captain, because an automatic reply can bury the context reminder and defeat the central purpose of this task.
Severity: moderate and directly relevant to the requested behavior.
Fix cost: likely final handoff checks in both paths plus deterministic late-arrival tests, checking all exit-2 branches rather than only the reported example; estimate 10-20 minutes before review.
Reviewer identifier: R1-2, P2; bin/fm-claude-stop-autoarm.sh:219 and bin/fm-turnend-guard.sh:155.
If guaranteeing the handoff requires shared coordination rather than extra checks, the fixer should explain that tradeoff before expanding the design.

Next round and recommendation
A fix-and-review round is estimated at 20-40 minutes including affected checks and a fresh independent review; this is an estimate, not a guarantee.
Continuing to fix both is worthwhile: the late-arrival defect defeats the requested hold, and the routed-message correction is small and uses existing infrastructure.
No fixer has been launched and neither finding has been fixed.
The captain decides whether to continue with both, select only one, or leave the implementation paused.

